docs/tooling/cost.md:79:an allocation promise that fails in debug builds. The report itself describes
SPEC.md:22:- [RECURSIVE-VALUES-1] A `record` or `union` may hold itself by value, directly, mutually, or through `Option`, tuples, fixed arrays, generic instances and other declarations, when the recursive type has a finite value: a union or `Option` case, or an empty fixed array, that ends every cycle. A cycle every value of which holds another value of it (`record Loop: next: Loop`) still rejects with `E_RECURSIVE_RECORD` at the field that closes it. A recursive value type cannot hold an owned resource, an unhandled result or a capability; each is `E_RECURSIVE_RECORD`. Recursion through `list`, `dict`, `set` and `class` needs no box and is unchanged.
SPEC.md:23:- [RECURSIVE-VALUES-3] Equality, dictionary and set hashing and key comparison, arena escape and collection walk a recursive value with growable work lists, so a value of any depth (a 1,000,000-node chain) compares, hashes and escapes without host recursion; equality compares each pair of boxes once, and list equality has no element-count limit. Printing a recursive value stops at the renderer's depth cap with `...`. The test boundary carries a recursive value as its typed JSON, and property tests generate recursive unions whose nesting grows with the run's size and shrink them toward their leaves.
SPEC.md:24:- [EQUALITY-SCRATCH-1] Native equality compares scalar values, text, bytes and small structural values without scratch allocations. Larger worklists and recursive visited-pair tables grow without a fixed depth or size limit; exhausted scratch allocation returns failure after releasing earlier buffers. Float equality keeps IEEE NaN and signed-zero behavior even inside a shared box or collection.
SPEC.md:40:- [OWN-11] Rejects direct and transitive allocation that violates declared effect bounds and reports a call witness.
SPEC.md:46:- [LIST-GROWTH-1] A list's backing is grown in place once it is big enough to own a whole segment, by the same `myl_native_heap_extend` a byte buffer grows with: the elements already stored are the ones the copy would have rewritten and the pages already faulted are the ones the copy would have faulted a second time for. Nothing outside the list holds a pointer into that backing — every element read, indexed write, sort, slice and dictionary entry derives its address from the header on the use that needs it — so the one value that cannot survive a move is an element pointer already inside the backing, which is exactly the source `myl_native_list_extend(list, list)` passes, and that value keeps the ordinary allocate-and-copy path. A backing the allocator cannot extend, an arena allocation, an immortal block and a request the budget refuses all fall back the same way, so values, lengths, capacities, identity, traps, traces and collection timing are unchanged.
SPEC.md:47:- [DICT-GETORPUT-1] A dictionary remembers where its last string-keyed probe landed and which key object it landed on, so the second half of a read-modify-write — `counts[k] = counts.get(k, 0) + 1` probes one key object twice in a row — answers with two pointer comparisons instead of another payload hash, table walk and content comparison. The record is a hint and never an answer: it is accepted only when the queried text pointer is the recorded one *and* the live entry at the recorded position still holds that same pointer, which identity proves to be the queried key, so an insertion, a `pop` that shifts every later entry down, a `clear`, a rebuilt index or a different text object with equal content each fall back to the full probe and reach the entry that probe reaches. A string key's miss is never recorded — a miss stays a miss until a write makes it a hit, which a stale record could not be allowed to shadow; [PY-DICT-4] extends the record to keys compared by value. Lookups, insertion order, defaults, traps and arena ownership are unchanged.
SPEC.md:48:- [BUFFER-GROWTH-1] A byte buffer's backing is grown in place once it is big enough to own a whole segment: `myl_native_heap_extend` hands the block's own reservation back to the host to resize, so the octets already written are the ones the copy would have rewritten and the pages already faulted are the ones the copy would have faulted a second time for. It answers NULL — leaving the block untouched — for a payload that is not the whole of its own one-slot segment (a size-class slot, an arena allocation, an immortal image block), for a count that is not larger, for one that would overflow, and for one the memory budget refuses after the same collection the ordinary allocation path runs; the caller then allocates a fresh block and copies. The block's element count and descriptor are rewritten exactly as `myl_native_heap_alloc` writes them, the heap is charged only the added stride, and the segment is relinked if the reservation moved. The bytes the block did not already hold are zeroed unless its element descriptor is one the collector never interprets — `MYL_NATIVE_PLAIN` or `MYL_NATIVE_TRACE_NONE`, exactly the test that keeps the tracing drain loop and `trace` off the payload — so an uninitialized tail can never be followed as a reference. A `byte_buffer` is the only grower: its backing is private, because `byte_buffer(...)` builds a fresh allocation or a copy and `view` refuses a mutable receiver, so no other reference can observe a moved reservation. Octets, lengths, capacities, identities, traps, traces and collection timing are unchanged.
SPEC.md:49:- [UTF8-TEXT-1] A `str` stores validated UTF-8 octets with a cached Unicode scalar count and an "every scalar is below 0x80" flag, so `len` and equality are one compare and every boundary that already holds UTF-8 — `from_utf8`, `utf8`, `bytes.encode`, a file path, a print sink — copies octets instead of decoding and re-encoding scalars. Scalar indexing, slicing, a stepped slice and `ord` keep Python's scalar semantics by computing byte offsets on demand: a scalar index is a byte index on ASCII text and one payload walk otherwise, and the same walk turns a byte offset back into a scalar index for `find`. Where scalars were compared, octets are: equality is a byte compare, order is UTF-8 byte order (which is code point order), `find`/`count`/`split`/`replace`/`splitlines`/`split()` scan octets because an ASCII scalar is its own octet and no octet of a non-ASCII scalar is below 0x80, case mapping and the three classifications stay ASCII-only, and rendering a nested text escapes per octet. A stepped slice gathers scalars, so it walks the payload once and copies the selected scalars' own octets rather than reading a stride of it, and the arena escape copier clones a text as one payload copy in the enclosing region because there is no pointer in it to patch. A whole-path file helper borrows a payload only for the write helpers that consume one, because `open`'s third operand is its mode spelling and the request's text bit describes the handle that mode names, not that operand. Immutability, UTF-8 validity, the `R_TEXT_SCALAR`, `R_TEXT_BOUNDS` and `R_EMPTY_SEPARATOR` traps, `dict[str, ...]` keys and every produced value are unchanged, and a text built from scalars outside `0..0x10FFFF` or inside the surrogate range is refused rather than stored. The header's fourth word is a payload-hash cache a dictionary fills on its first lookup of that text and no payload write can invalidate, so a read-modify-write of one key text hashes its octets once rather than once per lookup.
SPEC.md:50:- [RELEASE-CHECKED-1] No undefined behaviour in safe code, and optimization never changes a program's result: every build mode (Debug and ReleaseFast, `--release`), `mylang run`, `run-project`, `build --run` and `mylang test` emit the integer-overflow guards on add, subtract, multiply and unary negate, the narrowing integer-cast guards, the index-bounds guards on lists, fixed arrays, `bytes`, `str`, dictionary position reads and `pop`, and the frame protocol that reports a trap's trace, so a would-be trap is always reported, never undefined behaviour. A guard is omitted only where the compiler has proved it cannot fire (a range-fitting cast, a versioned loop's proved accesses and arithmetic). `--unchecked`, which once omitted them, is refused as removed: on `build` (with or without `--run`), `run`, `run-project` and `test` it is a usage error (exit 2) whose message says `--unchecked was removed: every build keeps overflow, cast and bounds checks so optimization never changes a program's result`, while the same spelling after `--` still reaches the guest.
SPEC.md:57:- [NATIVE-ZIG-CACHE-4] Every command `zig build` runs through its tool wrapper — the Zig test roots and their shards, and the Python and guest-test drivers — runs with `ZIG_LOCAL_CACHE_DIR` naming a fresh directory under `$TMPDIR` (else `/tmp`) made for that one command, replacing any value `zig build` inherited, and the directory is removed once the command's process has exited, however it ended; the command itself still replaces the wrapper, so it remains the build runner's direct child. A test or driver that runs `zig cc` itself from a scratch directory inside the checkout (`.zig-cache/tmp`, or a `$TMPDIR` there) therefore leaves no object in the project's `.zig-cache`, where Zig would otherwise have kept it under a key naming that scratch path. A test that needs the variable unset removes it from its child's environment.
SPEC.md:74:- [PROJ-1] Loads shared module dependencies once in dependency order and releases state on allocation failure.
SPEC.md:94:- [CHECK-LIBRARY-1] `mylang check-module FILE.my` checks a library module and its reachable imports, including ordinary and generic bodies, effects, public type boundaries and configured policy, warning and interface-file gates, without requiring or synthesizing an executable entry point. It accepts `--value-semantics` with the same checker rules as executable checks. It leaves no executable check verdict; `check-project`, build and run retain their main requirements. Libraries containing only type or generic declarations are valid.
SPEC.md:95:- [CHECK-LIBRARY-2] `mylang check FILE.my` checks a file that declares no free function `main` as a library, exactly as `check-module` does (bodies, effects, public type boundaries, configured warning and interface-file gates) and succeeds silently when it is well-typed, so the module an agent just edited needs no second command. This holds for a headerless single file and for a module or import entry, an empty file included; a type error in the library is reported as itself, never as `E_MAIN`. A file that declares `main` of any signature keeps the executable check, so a wrong signature, capability parameter list or generic `main` is still rejected with `E_MAIN` or `E_TYPE`. Commands that need an entry point (`run`, `build`, `check-project`, and `check --explain-ownership`, which analyzes a lowered program) keep `E_MAIN`, whose message names `mylang check FILE`. The library verdict is never cached as an executable one. `--diagnostics=json` reports the same diagnostics as text. `check` takes a file, not a directory. `check-module` is unchanged: it always checks as a library, even when `main` is declared, and requires a module header.
SPEC.md:122:- [STREAM-BYTES-1] `stream.write(data: bytes) -> int` writes the raw octets, including NUL and invalid UTF-8, without quoting or an added newline, flushes the selected stream, and returns the byte count. The existing `str` overload still returns its Unicode scalar count. Both preserve destination capability and the `output` effect, reject other argument types, and trap `R_OUTPUT` on a refused write or flush; byte output performs no intermediate text conversion or managed allocation.
SPEC.md:142:- [EQUALITY-CONTEXT-2] Equality context follows mixed field and list, dictionary or fixed-array index paths from a known binding, in either operand position, so `rows[i].items != []` and `[] == table[key]` infer the empty list's element type. Recovering the type does not evaluate an index or change left-to-right evaluation; two empty lists without a typed operand still report `E_LIST_INFERENCE`.
SPEC.md:152:- [EFFECT-LITERAL-1] A compile-time `str` literal contributes no `managed_allocation` in the typed effect walk, so `print("hi")` under `effects(output)` and `len("abc")` under `noalloc` are accepted; concatenation, slicing, conversion and `list`/`dict`/`bytes` literals still allocate.
SPEC.md:153:- [EFFECT-INDEX-1] An element read contributes `managed_allocation` only when its receiver is a `str`, whose element is a fresh one-character string; reading a `str` or `bytes` element out of a static array or dictionary hands back the stored reference and allocates nothing. The typed effect walk and the bytecode validator charge the read by the same receiver rule, so `names[0]` on a `[3]str` is accepted under `noalloc`, and a whole program that reads one lowers under `run`, `build` and `emit-project-qbe` instead of failing validation with an internal error.
SPEC.md:158:- [MUTATION-ENFORCE-1] A function cannot write caller-owned state through a read-only argument. Value transfers through records, arrays, options, unions, collections, helper results and wrappers are independent; local copies can be changed without changing their source. A `mut` argument writes through the caller's slot. Callee footprints substitute into actual arguments to a fixed point through recursion, unused generics, specializations and indirect callbacks; analysis-budget exhaustion widens conservatively with a located limitation. The guarantee is call-duration only and makes no total/trap-free/allocation-free/concurrency claim.
SPEC.md:160:- [INVALIDATION-EXECUTION-1] An invalidation report records actual query work: a stage a command did not run is reported `skipped`, never as reuse; lowering, emission and linking consume body and emitted-fragment inputs, so a body edit invalidates them; and a rejected check emits a best-effort explanation against the previous baseline without publishing a successful verdict.
SPEC.md:172:- [FRONTEND-CACHE-2] An ordinary one-shot `check-project` may reuse a successful whole-program verdict from the persistent cache only after loading every module fresh and comparing each module name, path and source byte against the content-addressed key; reused warnings are replayed and interface/semantic work is skipped. Failed checks are never stored, a compiler or diagnostic-format change misses, and `--report`/`--timings` expose reuse and miss counts.
SPEC.md:173:- [CACHE-CHECKSUM-1] Persisted frontend syntax and verdict entries and checked-body store entries carry a 64-bit payload checksum taken from the leading bytes of the payload's BLAKE3 digest; an entry whose payload no longer matches it is damaged and treated as a miss.
SPEC.md:177:- [CHECK-SCALE-LEX-1] Reserved words and removed spellings are recognised through compile-time maps, layout lowering finds line starts in one forward pass over the source, header discovery lexes each module into a scratch arena it resets before the next module (keeping only its diagnostics and import facts) and records which imports a dotted reference qualifies from the tokens it already holds (so the unused-import warning never lexes a module twice), and a reference spelling made only of identifier bytes and dots is normalized without lexing it. Every spelling lexes to the same tokens and every reference resolves exactly as through the full lexer: a keyword segment is still not a name, and a removed spelling is still left to the lexer, which reports `E_REMOVED_SPELLING`.
SPEC.md:181:- [SEMANTIC-KEY-TRANSITIVE-1] A declaration's dependency fingerprint is transitive and independent of the order declarations are keyed in: it covers its own digest and the own digest of every declaration it reaches, so a cycle of recursive or mutually recursive types folds every member into every member. A composite type spelling (`list[T]`, `dict[K, V]`, `Option[T]`, tuples, arrays, callback types) references each of its component types, a type declaration's own digest frames its source text (field types, defaults, `record`/`class`), and a name a facade re-exports binds the declaration it names. A field renamed, retyped or reshaped in a record reached only through a list element, a dictionary value, an Option payload, a return type, a type cycle or a facade therefore invalidates every body that reaches it, and an incremental check reports what a cold check reports.
SPEC.md:185:- [MODULE-INTERFACE-2] A module's interface hash covers its artifact except the positions: the declaration text without its blanked gaps, the imports, each function's stable key, role, export flag, facts and interface fingerprint (its declaration digest, role, facts and kept body, folded over every declaration those name in any module, in an order-independent way), each type's and re-export's fingerprint, and the shapes. An edit to a body the interface replaces that changes none of the module's exported facts leaves it unchanged, as do comments and blank lines between declarations, moved declarations and edits to private declarations nothing exported reaches; a signature, visibility, deprecation, type declaration, record shape (including a record reached only through `list[T]`, `Option[T]`, a dictionary or a union payload of another module), fact, template, constant or inlined body changes it. The implementation hash is the module's name and source bytes and the interface hash of each direct import, so a body edit changes only its own module's. Neither hash contains a path or a time.
SPEC.md:189:- [INCREMENTAL-KEYS-1] Every incremental cache entry includes the compiler build identity, bootstrap-language version, target, runtime ABI, effects/capabilities contract and relevant flags through one contract fingerprint. Changing any dimension invalidates parsed syntax, whole-program verdicts, checked function bodies, generic-specialization keys and native object fragments even when the source bytes are identical.
SPEC.md:220:- [GENERIC-INSTANCE-1] The compilation-local specialization registry interns resolved declaration identities and ordered concrete type identities, owns argument storage, preserves IDs across growth and allocation failure, and reuses recursive requests. It bounds discovery at 4096 instances per generic declaration (GENERIC-INSTANCE-BUDGET-1) and each argument list at 16. Nominal types remain distinct regardless of representation. Executable checking uses these identities without conflating them with source declaration ordinals.
SPEC.md:268:- [CHECK-CACHE-1] A persistent checking session may reuse a successful verdict only after fresh project and manifest loading and exact comparison of every loaded module name, path and source byte. Hits replay owned warnings and skip interface/semantic work; failures are never retained. Retention is bounded and clear drops syntax and verdict caches while preserving cumulative observations.
SPEC.md:280:- [GENERIC-CLEANUP-1] Concrete resource ownership passed through generic functions retains its provider lifecycle. Explicit partial extraction/drop and implicit scope cleanup release each live resource exactly once, including allocation failures and traps; forwarding does not complete or duplicate an external resource.
SPEC.md:307:- [NOMINAL-PROVIDER-1] Generic resource/environment operations accept symbolic nominal result and payload records only when their exact named fields satisfy the ordinary provider contract for every permitted substitution. Declaration order does not affect meaning. Byte inputs and output arrays must have known u8 elements and numeric lengths; a general integer parameter is insufficient. Concrete and symbolic checking share named contract definitions. Resource identity, authority, mutable access, handling, cleanup and effect checks remain enforced before calls. VM specialization preserves bounded bytes, absent environment values, partial I/O, failed-completion ownership and cleanup under allocation failure or instruction traps.
SPEC.md:312:- [GENERIC-BODY-3] A generic body, and every non-generic function it calls, may sort a concrete list in place with `values.sort()` when the element is an integer, a float or `str`, the same set the concrete checker accepts: the abstract checker types the call as unit and records it as a write to its receiver, as it does `append`, but seeds no allocation, because a sort allocates nothing managed. A list whose element is a type parameter or any other type still rejects with `E_COLLECTION_METHOD`, and every other unsupported collection operation keeps `E_GENERIC_BODY_UNSUPPORTED`.
SPEC.md:313:- [GENERIC-BODY-4] A generic body may sort any list in place with `values.sort(key=key, reverse=flag)`, on a named receiver or any expression: the abstract checker checks the operands in source order, `reverse` as a `bool`, records a write to the receiver and seeds `managed_allocation` for the key list, and leaves the key to each specialization, which checks it against its concrete element type under LIST-SORT-KEY-2. Without `key=` the element still needs a native order, as for GENERIC-BODY-3, and a positional or unknown operand is `E_CALL_ARGUMENT`. A generic body may also call the prelude `sorted(values, key=..., reverse=...)` when no binding or declaration claims the name, typed as its list argument, with the same operand rules and `managed_allocation`, which is how `sort.sort_by_key` forwards; and it may take `values.copy()` of a list, typed as the list and seeding `managed_allocation`.
SPEC.md:347:- [STANDARD-BYTE-BUILDER-1] The standard `byte_builder` module assembles an immutable `bytes` snapshot over one mutable `byte_buffer` ([PY-BYTES-6]), so `append`, `append_range`, `append_byte`, `append_spaces`, `append_text`, `append_int` and `append_float` write each fragment's octets straight into that buffer's capacity and no fragment allocates a value for itself, with `append_float` writing the canonical spelling `str(value)` prints. The checked appends reject before writing anything, so a refused call leaves the builder byte-for-byte unchanged: `append_range` rejects a negative `start`, an `end` below `start` or an `end` past `len(data)` and accepts an empty range that changes nothing, `append_byte` rejects values outside `0..255`, and `append_spaces` and `reserve` reject negative counts. `reserve` grows capacity for a further `extra` octets without changing the finished length, and `finish` publishes the buffer as an immutable snapshot in append order without copying its octets ([PY-BYTES-SNAPSHOT-1]): it contributes `managed_allocation` but no mutation, the builder stays usable, and later appends, including an append of a snapshot back into its own builder, cannot change an earlier result. Every operation keeps the module's published names, argument types, effects and results, carries `mutation` and `managed_allocation` where it writes, mutates only its builder argument, and leaves the buffer traced, so a builder survives collection at every managed allocation.
SPEC.md:363:- [AFFECTED-TEST-1] `mylang test --affected --against REF` derives its selection from the working-tree diff rather than from the standing tree: it maps the changed lines `git` reports for `REF` onto the declarations they touch ([AFFECTED-TEST-2]) and runs a discovered test only when a changed declaration is in the dependency closure the run already derives for the test's receipt. It is a selection and never a verdict: the selected count and every skipped test are printed, `--filter` still applies first, `lint --gate` and CI run the whole set, and an empty derivation exits 3 exactly as a filter matching nothing does. `--against` without `--affected` (and `--affected` without `--against`) is a usage error, and a `REF` `git` cannot resolve is a host failure (status 2) rather than an empty selection. A project that cannot be checked fails exactly as it does without `--affected`, and a declared skip, an undefaultable case or a test the checked graph cannot name is kept rather than silently skipped.
SPEC.md:367:- [TEST-CAPS-1] Deterministic stand-in capabilities are built by effect-free standard functions that need no capability: `time.simulated_clock(start: Instant) -> Clock` reads `start` until `time.advance(clock: Clock, duration: Duration) -> Result[None, str]` moves it (copies share one reading; a host-granted clock is `Err("only a simulated clock can be advanced")`; leaving the signed nanosecond range is `Err` and leaves the reading unchanged); `random.seeded_entropy(seed: int) -> Entropy` yields the SplitMix64 stream the host's `--allow-random=SEED` uses, so `random.from_entropy` on equal seeds is reproducible and each read advances the stream; `filesystem.memory() -> Filesystem` is an empty in-memory tree that serves every whole-path helper, `open` in `r`/`w`/`a` modes with the handle methods, `exists`, `mkdir`, `remove` (a file or an empty directory) and name-sorted `listdir`, with the scoped-root path rules and the host surface's error texts; `environment.fixed(values: dict[str, str]) -> Environment` answers `get`, `names` and `only` from exactly those pairs (a name that is empty, longer than 128 bytes or holds `=` traps `R_ENVIRONMENT`); and `network.denied() -> Network` refuses every acquisition, and every `net.host(...)` narrowing of it, with the `Connected.err` code `network.denied_here()` (6), which `network.describe(code)` spells `network access is denied in this context: this Network is a deterministic stand-in`. `mylang test` accepts a program that imports `network`: the declaration contracts render its `resource Connection: network` family as written instead of resolving it as a type.
SPEC.md:371:- [PROPERTY-TEST-1] A discovered `test_` function with data parameters and no cases-file `arguments` is a property test: `mylang test` generates an input for every data parameter from its checked type, supplies the deterministic stand-ins for its capability parameters as for any test, and passes when every one of `--property-runs N` inputs (default 100, at most 1,000,000) returns `True` or `Ok(True)` without trapping; `False`, `Ok(False)`, `Err(...)` and any trap other than a discard fail it. Inputs are a pure function of the seed — the test's `Entropy` seed, the SHA-256 prefix of its qualified name unless `--seed N` names one — and the run number, drawn by a SplitMix64 stream per run; run sizes grow from 0 on the first run to 100 on the last (a collection holds at most the size at the top level and half of it per level down, and an input stops growing collections past 4096 nodes). Integers draw three in eight from the edges of their width (0, 1, -1, the minimum and maximum, a power of two, one below it and its negation), one in eight uniformly over the width and the rest near zero; `bool`, `float` (both zeros, both infinities, NaN, the extreme finite and subnormal magnitudes, and small binary fractions), `str` (seven scalars in ten printable ASCII, plus controls, well-known non-ASCII scalars and scalars from the whole range), `bytes`, `list[T]`, `dict[K, V]` and `set[T]` (a repeated key or element is dropped), tuples, fixed arrays, enum-indexed tables, `Option[T]`, `Result[T, E]`, records fieldwise and unions casewise all generate. A parameter whose type reaches a `class`, a `domain` (other than `float`), a function value, `Host` or a type the schema does not describe is a configuration error that fails the test naming the parameter, its type and the reason, and so is a property test whose result is not `bool` or `Result[bool, E]`. A cases-file entry with `arguments` still runs the test with exactly those arguments.
SPEC.md:373:- [PROPERTY-TEST-3] The first failing input is shrunk greedily: each parameter in turn is replaced by the first of its simpler candidates, tried in order, that still fails (for any reason other than a discard), until a pass over every parameter changes nothing or 2,000 candidates have run. Integers shrink toward 0 (0, then the positive of a negative, then halving the distance: 100 offers 0, 50, 75, 88, 94, 97, 99); floats toward `0.0` by their whole part, halved exponents and the integer series; strings, bytes, lists, sets and dictionaries by dropping every element, then halves, quarters and single elements, then (for integer lists) moving an element's value into its neighbour, then every slot shrunk together and one at a time breadth first — never into a set element or dictionary key another slot holds; records and tuples fieldwise; enums and unions toward earlier cases at their simplest; `Option` toward `None`. The failure reports `falsified after N passing runs (seed S)`, the minimal input as one mylang literal per parameter (`xs = [100]`, `Point(x=49, y=1i32)`, `Some("aaaa")`, `0.0 / 0.0`), the original input with its run number, the reason (`returned False`, `returned Err(...)` followed by that input's error return trace (ERROR-TRACE-3), or a trap's code, message and frames), the original input's own reason when it differs, and `reproduce: mylang test ENTRY --filter 'NAME' --seed S`; an input that ran past `--timeout-ms` is reported unshrunk. `--json` adds a `property` object to every property test's result: `seed`, `counts` (`runs`, `passed`, `discarded`, `processes`, `shrinks`, `attempts`) and, for a falsified one, `failure` with the minimal and original inputs as typed JSON `arguments` and `literals`, the run, the reason and the reproduction.
SPEC.md:375:- [PROPERTY-TEST-5] The copied-value codec boundary carries `float` as one scalar node of its IEEE bits (typed JSON: the shortest decimal with a `.`, or `inf`, `-inf`, `nan`), `bytes` as kind `bytes` with one `u8` node per octet, and a dictionary or a set of any hashable key as kind `dict` naming its key and value (a set's value is unit and carries no node): one node counting the entries, then each entry's key tree and, for a dictionary, its value tree, in iteration order (typed JSON: an array of `[key, value]` pairs, or of elements). A repeated key in a request is refused as `InvalidArgument`. The checked schema names a dictionary's or a set's `key`, and marks a `class` record with `class: true`.
SPEC.md:382:- [MEMORY-CEILING-1] Every process that runs guest code runs under a byte ceiling. The soft ceiling is the managed heap's own budget: an allocation that would exceed it after a collection traps with `R_MEMORY_BUDGET`, "memory budget exhausted", and exit status 1, rather than growing. The ceiling travels in `MYLANG_MEMORY_LIMIT` as bytes with an optional `K`, `M` or `G` suffix, so no spawner needs a new argument ABI and a program started by hand under an exported variable is bounded the same way; malformed text refuses the run instead of leaving it unbounded, and zero removes the ceiling. `--max-memory=BYTES` on `run`, `build --run` and `test` outranks the variable, which outranks the built-in default: 512 MiB for one test child, 4 GiB for `run` and `build --run`. A resolved test ceiling is part of a receipt key, so changing it re-executes rather than reuses.
SPEC.md:388:- [MEMORY-CEILING-2] Behind the soft ceiling the runtime caps its own address space with `RLIMIT_AS` at the budget plus fixed headroom, before its first allocation, so a runtime defect cannot outgrow the budget. It never loosens a tighter ceiling already in force and installs none under a sanitizer, whose shadow mapping reserves more address space than any budget allows. The compiler bounds itself the same way at start-up, at 8 GiB unless `MYLANG_HOST_MEMORY_LIMIT` says otherwise, and the tools a native build spawns complete under the inherited ceiling. Every host that runs a guest in its own process — the byte runner, the board trial and the schematic renderer — installs that same ceiling before it runs anything, because there a runaway guest is a runaway host.
SPEC.md:390:- [ARENA-BOUNDED-1] A project's arena (every module's source, syntax and checked program) holds no more untouched address space than one 64 MiB chunk: shared chunks grow by half up to that size, and an allocation of 4 MiB or more gets its own mapping from the child allocator, which a `remap` grows in place of a copy and a `free` returns. The compiler's address-space ceiling (`MYLANG_HOST_MEMORY_LIMIT`, 8 GiB) therefore bounds what the frontend touches, not a geometric reservation: `std.heap.ArenaAllocator`'s half-again node growth had a cold million-line check fail with 4.2 GB resident when its next node would have crossed the ceiling. A module's tokens, which nothing reads after its parse, are lexed from the child allocator and freed at once rather than kept in the project arena. The arena's state is shared by copies of its owner and is single-threaded.
SPEC.md:472:- [CONSTANT-1] Module-level const NAME: TYPE = EXPR declarations define immutable builtin integer or bool values. Initializers use literals, scalar casts/operators and visible constants with acyclic forward dependencies; allocation and external effects are excluded even in untaken operands; SOURCE-EVAL-1 adds bounded scalar source-function calls. Checking evaluates with width/overflow/division validation and bounded depth/work before emitting literal getters. Normal short-circuit evaluation applies after syntax/dependency validation. Exports and imported names use ordinary privacy checks; generic functions may read constants.
SPEC.md:473:- [CONSTANT-2] Module-level str and bytes constants require a matching text or byte literal initializer, including empty literals and ordinary escapes; calls other than `embed_bytes`/`embed_str` ([EMBED-1]), concatenation, casts and dependencies are rejected even when unused. Transparent aliases resolve normally. Their getters retain ordinary checked literals and contribute managed_allocation on every read, including imported and generic reads; noalloc/effects() callers reject. Scalar constant evaluation is unchanged. Checking, run, build --run and installed executables agree, and literal edits invalidate cached semantics and emitted objects.
SPEC.md:484:- [RUNTIME-BYTES-1] Embedded allocation budgets support child quotas clamped to the parent limit. Child allocations and resize/remap operations charge both live-byte limits; failed acquisitions do not charge storage; release restores live accounting and retains peak statistics. Accounting objects must outlive every allocation made through them.
SPEC.md:506:- [SEMANTIC-CACHE-1] Supported changed-source checks reuse independently owned checked function bodies with relocated spans, nodes, statements and locals; failed checks or snapshot allocation failures do not replace the last complete snapshot.
SPEC.md:509:- [SEMANTIC-CACHE-7] Ordinary checked-body eligibility is per function: an unrelated generic, alias, constant or callback declaration no longer disables reuse, and changing one ordinary body rechecks that body while unchanged dependents reuse. Entries are keyed by stable declaration identity with dependency digests over referenced declarations, types, layouts, constants, scope/import/visibility lookups including negative lookups, and effects/mutation/return-origin contracts. Old function and record ordinals remap to the fresh catalog; missing or ambiguous remapping is a miss, never a partial checked body. Global effect, mutation and codec passes still rerun after every body edit so a reused caller never keeps an obsolete callee verdict.
SPEC.md:515:- [SEMANTIC-CACHE-14] Ordinary checked bodies, symbolic templates and concrete specializations share one `reuse.max_bytes` in-memory budget that accounts for owned entry rows, exact arena backing allocations including per-chunk bookkeeping, stable keys, and exact hash-index backing capacity including the stdlib minimum capacity, header and alignment. Newly checked publication and durable-hit promotion converge on the bound by releasing the globally oldest generic entry or truncating the ordinary snapshot to its fitting leading prefix; evicted entries miss and check fresh, oversized entries bypass retention without changing acceptance, a zero budget disables reuse, and `functions.retained_bytes` reports the total with a per-kind breakdown and eviction count.
SPEC.md:520:- [SEMANTIC-CACHE-4] Reusing a caller body never reuses transitive effect verdicts; a callee's new effect rejects an unchanged effect-bounded caller.
SPEC.md:526:- [CALLBACK-SLOT-1] An unannotated function used as a value takes the effect ceiling of the callback slot it fills: a parameter known at the call, and equally a generic callee's callback parameter once its type arguments are inferred from the checked arguments (`sort.sort_by_key(xs, key)` with `key` unannotated). The function's inferred transitive body effects must fit that ceiling: a function value whose target has no clause of its own and whose body reaches an effect the slot forbids — `managed_allocation` included, so an allocating helper no longer fills an `effects()` slot a `noalloc` caller invokes — is `E_EFFECT_BOUND` at the value, naming the target, the slot type, the effect and its witness path, so no effect can reach a bounded caller through a callback. `mutation` is the mutation analysis's, an unbounded slot accepts any body, and a sort key that builds a `str` still fits because the key slot admits allocation (LAMBDA-KEY-INFER-1).
SPEC.md:533:- [STANDARD-TIME-RANDOM-2] Standard clock and entropy calls require explicit capabilities and keep the unavailable, denied and failed provider categories apart in their reported messages; a later failed read leaves earlier results intact and host allocation failure aborts normally.
SPEC.md:534:- [STANDARD-TIME-RANDOM-3] Standard provider wrappers preserve source effect ceilings, so a caller declaring a narrower ceiling than clock or random plus managed allocation is rejected while checking; one entropy-seeded generator performs exactly one provider read and replays identically from its state word.
SPEC.md:555:- [STANDARD-SORT-KEY-1] `sort.sort_by_key(values, key)` is a thin forwarder to `sorted(values, key=key)` (LIST-SORT-KEY-1), so it returns a new stable list and leaves its input unchanged. Each key is computed exactly once, in input order — earlier versions computed them in the order a recursive merge first asked for each one, so a key function that traps on several elements now raises the trap of the first such element in input order, as Python does. The extra memory is the result, one key word per element and the native sort's scratch, for lists of any length.
SPEC.md:589:- [GC-ROOTS-1] Managed collections and aggregate snapshots retain independent values across later mutations. Retained values, including intermediate call arguments and aggregate snapshots, root managed objects across allocation and stress collection.
SPEC.md:592:- [GC-SOURCE-3] Managed results held as intermediate call arguments or inside ordinary record snapshots survive collection triggered by later arguments and callee allocations.
SPEC.md:594:- [GC-SOURCE-6] `noalloc` rejects transitive managed allocation and list growth; fixed-slot mutation and reads remain permitted.
SPEC.md:626:- [IO-1] `stream.print(values...)` writes each argument's Python rendering to the receiver stream's destination, separated by single spaces and followed by one newline. `int` and the fixed-width integers render in decimal, `bool` as `True`/`False`, `None` as `None`, `float` with a `.0` suffix when it has no fractional digits and `nan`/`inf`/`-inf` for the nonfinite values, `str` raw at the top level and `repr`-quoted inside a container, and `bytes` as a Python `b'...'` literal with `\n`, `\r`, `\t`, `\'`, `\\` and `\xNN` escapes. Lists render as `[a, b]`, insertion-ordered dictionaries as `{k: v}` and fixed arrays as `[a, b]`. Nesting deeper than 24 levels renders as `...`, so a cyclic managed graph still terminates. `stream.print()` writes only the newline.
SPEC.md:635:- [IO-11] A file `with` scope closes its handle exactly once at every exit: falling off the end, `return`, `?` propagation, `break`, `continue`, an explicit `f.close()`, and a trap or host allocation failure unwinding through it. A method on a closed or stale handle traps with `R_FILE`; so does any provider failure during a method, since `f.read()` yields bytes rather than a result. The trap message carries the same readable reason as the `Result` error text.
SPEC.md:637:- [IO-13] A `class` value renders its own fields wherever it is nested — a union or option payload, a record or class field, a tuple or fixed-array element, a list or dictionary element — exactly as it renders at the top level. Every generated renderer receives the address of its value's slot, and a class slot holds the body pointer, so the class renderer loads that pointer before reading any field; no renderer reads a field at the slot itself.
SPEC.md:646:- [REQUIRED-PROJECT-IMPORT-POLICY-1] A `mylang.json` discovered by walking up from the entry source file to the filesystem root makes its `import_policy` required for ordinary `check`, `check-project`, `build`, `run`, `run-project`, project variants and native test preparation, evaluated before any cached success and never selected from the shell working directory. Missing or malformed configuration is a host failure (status 2); a policy violation is status 3 and language or import-cycle errors stay status 1. Policy and configuration identity participates in verdict caches and receipts so a stricter policy invalidates an earlier pass, import-cycle checking cannot be disabled, and projects without a configuration keep their prior behavior.
SPEC.md:647:- [POLICY-IDENTITY-OWNED-1] The configured-policy identity that the successful-verdict caches key on is copied into memory each cache owns when it is published, and freed when the cache is dropped, so a warm `check-project` or `check-session` request under a `mylang.json` replays its stored verdict exactly as an unconfigured project does instead of keying on stale stack bytes. Every request first clears the identity, so a long-lived session whose project stops configuring one never keys on the previous request's configuration.
SPEC.md:648:- [TESTS-FOR-1] `mylang tests-for <entry.my> <qualified-name>` loads and checks the project, then prints the discovered tests that reach that declaration: a `direct` list of tests whose body calls it directly, and a `reached-by` list of every test that reaches it transitively. It is a selection aid, not a verdict: an unknown qualified name prints a message and exits 2, and a declaration no test reaches prints empty lists and exits 0.
SPEC.md:655:- [API-FILE-3] A `mylang.json` carrying `"interface_files": true` makes the interface files part of checking the project: once its bodies check, `check`, `check-project`, `run`, `run-project` and a whole-program `emit-project-qbe` (the `build` frontend) report each missing or stale file as `E_INTERFACE_FILE_STALE` at `PATH:1:1`, naming the module and the `mylang api <entry> --write` to run, and exit 1. Every checked-in interface file's bytes are folded into the configuration identity the successful-verdict caches key on, so editing or deleting one never replays an earlier success. The value must be a JSON boolean; without the key, or with `false`, nothing changes. Navigation, listing, lint and native-test preparation do not enforce it.
SPEC.md:656:- [LINT-2] A `mylang.json` version 2 may carry a `lint` object: `profile` (`agent` default, `strict`, `safety`), `on_build` (`report` default, `block`), `warnings` (`report` default, `deny`), positive integer `min_chars` (the `undocumented-export` docstring floor, 12 by default), `block`/`ratchet`/`report` arrays of rule names, and a `rules` object whose per-rule settings carry positive integer `max`, `hard_max` and `recover_pct`, reasoned `exempt` and `allow` entries, and `modules` globs. Unknown or duplicate keys, malformed values, a non-object `lint`, and an exemption without a name or a non-empty reason are located configuration failures (status 2); `lint` requires `version: 2`, and a version 1 document keeps parsing without it. A configured `lint` object participates in verdict cache identity exactly as `import_policy` does.
SPEC.md:692:- [MODULE-DOT-4] Dotted module parsing, namespace normalization, checking, and execution release all allocations on allocation failure.
SPEC.md:713:- [PYTHON-1] Sources use `def`/`record`/`class` heads, colon and space-indented blocks, `#` comments, implicit continuation inside delimiters, and source-preserving virtual block delimiters. A suite opens at the `:` that ends its line and closes at its dedent; `{` and `}` are only a dictionary literal, so a brace-bodied declaration is rejected. A suite's reported bytes end at the last token it wrote, so the blank lines and comments before the next declaration belong to neither, and a function's measured length and navigation excerpt cover only the function. Missing blocks, inconsistent dedents, and indentation tabs are diagnosed. Indentation is the only block syntax, so `fmt` returns the text a program already has.
SPEC.md:738:- [PRELUDE-EXTREME-1] `min` and `max` take a list of `int`, `float` or `str` values, or two scalars of one type, and answer an element. Text order compares the stored Unicode scalars directly and contributes no managed allocation, so a text extrema fold is accepted under `noalloc`. An empty list has no smallest element, so it traps `R_LIST_BOUNDS` at the call site.
SPEC.md:747:- [PRELUDE-KEYS-1] `list`, `sorted`, `sum`, `min`, `max`, `any` and `all` of a dictionary read its keys, and of a `keys()` or `values()` view that view's snapshot, in insertion order as a `for` loop visits them; a set reads its elements the same way ([SET-1]).
SPEC.md:749:- [PY-COMPREHENSION-1] `[value for name in iterable if condition]` builds a fresh list through one checked loop over a list, string, byte string or dictionary, under the same fixed iteration bound and mutation check as `for`. An unfiltered comprehension reserves that bound before filling the list; a filtered comprehension retains ordinary growth so a sparse result does not reserve the full source size. The filter runs before the value, the binding lives only inside the comprehension, and the source collection is unchanged.
SPEC.md:750:- [PY-COMPREHENSION-2] A one-clause, one-name list comprehension rejects a binding that shadows an active name, a non-Boolean filter, a source that is not a managed collection or an integer range, and a produced value that is not a copyable storable list element. A filter, two iteration names, several `for` clauses, dict comprehensions and generator expressions lower through their own loops instead ([PY-GATHER-1], [PY-GENERATOR-1]).
SPEC.md:753:- [PY-GATHER-1] A list or set comprehension with a filter, two iteration names or a second `for` clause, and every dict comprehension `{k: v for ...}`, lowers through ordinary loops: each `for` clause is the `for` statement it spells — so it accepts exactly what a `for` loop accepts, including `range` with a step, `enumerate`, `zip`, `reversed`, dictionary views and `for k, v in d.items()` — each `if` clause wraps what follows it — so a filter narrows the `Option` value it tests, as an `if` statement does — clauses nest left to right as Python reads them, and the innermost step appends, adds or writes one value. Every binding is confined to the comprehension. A dict comprehension evaluates the key before the value, and a repeated key keeps its first position and its last value; a set comprehension keeps each element's first position. An annotated destination supplies the element, key and value types to their literals. A one-clause, one-name list comprehension without a filter keeps its reserving form ([PY-COMPREHENSION-1]), and the same set comprehension its dedicated form ([SET-1]). A set comprehension's elements must be hashable ([HASH-KEYS-1]), as must a dict comprehension's keys.
SPEC.md:754:- [PY-GENERATOR-1] `f(x for x in xs)` passes a generator expression as the sole argument, and `(x for x in xs)` is one anywhere a parenthesized argument is. `sum`, `any`, `all`, `min` and `max` fold its values through its own loops without building a list: `sum` starts from zero of the value's `int` or `float` type, `any` and `all` leave every loop of the generator at the first `True` or `False`, and `min`/`max` of no values trap `R_ASSERT` with CPython's `min() arg is an empty sequence`. `sorted`, `list` and `str.join` receive the list the loop builds, `set` the set, and `dict` the dictionary of its `(key, value)` tuples. A generator anywhere else — stored in a variable, passed to a declared function or beside a second positional argument — is `E_GENERATOR`, with the list comprehension as its alternative.
SPEC.md:757:- [PY-FSTRING-1] `f"..."` interpolates `{expression}` holes and constructs the pieces into one `str` with one exactly-sized result allocation rather than a chain of growing-prefix concatenations. A single piece returns its rendered text directly without a join or intermediate list. Holes evaluate and render exactly once from left to right. A hole takes any value `print` can render — lists, dictionaries, records, variants, results, options and byte strings as well as scalars — and renders it through the same renderer, so one value has one spelling wherever it is written. A hole holds one ordinary expression, including calls, operators, indexing and a nested format string, and is checked in its own source position. `f` before a quote is the prefix; anywhere else it is an ordinary identifier.
SPEC.md:758:- [PY-FSTRING-2] `{{` and `}}` stand for single braces, ordinary string escapes apply to the literal pieces, and an empty format string is empty text. An empty hole, an unmatched or unterminated brace, more than one expression in a hole, a format specification its value cannot take (`PY-FSTRING-SPEC-2`), and a value with no rendering — a capability, an open file or a callback — are all rejected. Rendering releases its partial buffer on allocation failure and reports it as `OutOfMemory`, the way every other allocating writer in the compiler does.
SPEC.md:762:- [PY-FOR-REBIND-1] A `for` loop variable is rebound from the loop at the top of every iteration, exactly as in Python, so assigning it in the body — `i = 10`, `i += 3`, a destructuring target `i, x = pair` — changes the variable for the rest of that iteration and never which iteration comes next or how many run. This holds for every loop form: `range(end)` and `range(start, end)`, both names of `enumerate`, the element of a list, string, bytes, dictionary, dictionary view or fixed array, `reversed`, both names of `zip` and of a two-value tuple or dictionary items, nested loops, and the same forms in a generic function body or a compile-time function's `range` loop. A counting loop — `range` or an `enumerate` position — whose body never assigns its variable counts on that variable itself, which is then immutable, so its lowering is unchanged and `PY-ARRAY-NEGATIVE-1` may treat it as never negative; a body that can assign it (read from the syntax before the body is checked: an assignment, annotated binding or destructuring target naming it, or a place write rooted at it) gets its own mutable variable, copied from a hidden counter at the top of each iteration and not treated as non-negative. `continue` still advances the hidden counter; `break` still skips it.
SPEC.md:766:- [PY-STR-2] `replace(old, new)`, `find(sub)` (-1 when absent), `count(sub)`, `startswith`, `endswith`, `isdigit`, `isalpha` and `isspace` follow Python's answers, including `False` for every classification of empty text. Empty search/count needles follow PY-TEXT-RANGE-1; an empty split separator or replace needle traps `R_EMPTY_SEPARATOR`, and these methods are rejected on lists and dictionaries.
SPEC.md:778:- [PY-ARRAY-WRITE-1] A fixed array `[N]T` or enum table `[Kind]T` is written in place with the list spelling: `a[i] = v`, `a[i] op= v`, `a[i].f = v`, `a[i].f.g = v`, `a[i][j] = v` for an array of arrays, `r.cells[i] = v` through a value record, `c.cells[i] = v` through a `class`, and `xs[k][j] = v` or `xs[k].cells[j] = v` below a list or dictionary slot. The write is `a = replace(a, i, v)` for each array step and a record update for each field step, stored back into the binding the path starts at — or into the last list or dictionary slot on the path, which is a reference — so value semantics are those of `replace`: `b = a` copies, and a copy taken before the write keeps its value. The binding must be mutable (`E_IMMUTABLE` otherwise) and copyable. The assigned value is evaluated before the target's indices, an index is evaluated once even though it both reads and rebuilds the path, and a value that could write the binding (a call) is computed before the binding is read, so nothing it writes is lost. An index follows the fixed-array index rule of `PY-ARRAY-NEGATIVE-1` exactly as a read and `replace` do: a negative index counts from the end, and an index outside `-N <= i < N` traps `R_LIST_BOUNDS` with the index as written and the length at the write's source position. A write whose path ends inside a `str` or `bytes`, or that is rooted anywhere other than a binding or a list or dictionary slot, keeps its existing diagnostic.
SPEC.md:783:- [PY-TABLE-1] A dict literal whose expected type is an enum-indexed table `[Kind]T` constructs that table, its keys spelled `Kind.case` and its values arranged in enum layout order independently of source order.
SPEC.md:795:- [PY-COLLECTIONS-6] Generic list literals, iteration, slicing and writes preserve symbolic element types and allocation effects.
SPEC.md:796:- [PY-COLLECTIONS-7] Receiver calls, computed indexes and assignment values run exactly once in source order and survive collection at every allocation.
SPEC.md:818:- [PY-COLLECTIONS-20] Reading an absent list value (`index` or `remove`) traps with `R_LIST_VALUE`, popping an empty list traps with `R_LIST_BOUNDS`, and popping an absent dictionary key without a default traps with `R_MISSING_KEY`; no absent value is silently replaced by a sentinel.
SPEC.md:822:- [PY-AUGASSIGN-2] An augmented assignment writes through a list slot or dictionary entry: `values[index] op= value` and `counts[key] op= value` read the slot once, evaluate the assigned value first, apply the operator's rules to the element and write the result back through the same receiver and index, which are each evaluated once. The element type of `str` values concatenates and integer or `float` elements take their own operators.
SPEC.md:825:- [CANONICAL-LEN-1] `len(value)` returns `int` for lists, dictionaries, strings, bytes and fixed arrays. The removed `.len()` member spelling reports `E_REMOVED_SPELLING` at the member and names `len(value)` as its replacement.
SPEC.md:827:- [PRELUDE-TYPES-2] The nominal catalog always registers `str`, `dict`, `bytes` and `float` unless a program declares that type name. Registration depends on parsed declarations and AST use, never on scanning raw source words or comment-like operator text.
SPEC.md:834:- [PY-REPEAT-1] `sequence * count` and `count * sequence` repeat a `str`, a `bytes` or a list by an integer of any width, as Python does: a count of zero or less answers an empty value, a repeated list holds an independent copy of each source element, as every list store does under value semantics, both operands are evaluated once in source order, and a repeated `str` or `bytes` is joined into one exactly-sized allocation. `*=` repeats a `str` or list binding in place of the binding.
SPEC.md:836:- [STDLIB-TEXT-SCALAR-1] The `strings` module exposes `ord(text)` and `chr(code)`: `ord` returns the Unicode scalar value of a single-character text, `chr` returns that one-character text for a scalar value, both reject any other length, and `chr` rejects surrogates and code points past `0x10FFFF` with the module's `Invalid` or `Overflow` reason. Both are total `Result` values under the module's declared `managed_allocation` effect.
SPEC.md:837:- [PY-DICT-1] Managed dictionaries preserve insertion order and independent values across assignments, compare keys by value ([HASH-KEYS-1]), distinguish missing keys from defaults, trace key/value cycles, and fail allocation or capacity growth atomically. Managed data equality compares contents with cycle detection; dictionary equality ignores insertion order. Membership searches dictionary keys, list values, or a text substring. Python in/not in evaluate the needle before the container exactly once. Dictionary iteration returns keys in insertion order and traps if the iterated dictionary changes size; generic dictionary literals preserve symbolic key/value types.
SPEC.md:838:- [DICT-VIEWS-1] Dictionary keys, values and items produce nonallocating ephemeral typed views for direct iteration, comprehensions and immediate len, membership, list, set and sorted consumption. Items yield DictItem key/value records and support two-name unpacking. Iteration observes value replacements and rejects dictionary size changes. list(d.keys()), list(d.values()) and list(d.items()) create independent snapshots in insertion order, including generic checking.
SPEC.md:839:- [D2-VIEW-STORAGE-1] Builtin dictionary-view types are rejected at parameter, return, record-field and collection-element declarations, including unused generic declarations. A dictionary view expression cannot be bound, rebound, stored in any aggregate or collection, returned, discarded, projected through its mapping field or passed to a user function (including a mut argument). Concrete and symbolic generic checking reject these uses with E_VIEW_STORED. Certain snapshot migrations carry a structured list(...) fix around the exact keys, values or items expression; direct iteration and immediate len, in, list, set and sorted remain legal. User declarations that shadow builtin view names remain ordinary values.
SPEC.md:840:- [LIVE-VIEW-ACYCLIC-1] The defensive internal catalog check rejects recursive storage cycles through compiler-constructed dictionary-view referents with E_LIVE_VIEW_CYCLE. Its iterative walk follows record fields, variant payloads, nonempty fixed arrays and collection keys/elements using builtin declaration identity. D2-VIEW-STORAGE-1 rejects source view annotations before layout checking, including phantom arguments and empty arrays; snapshots, ordinary recursive values and user declarations with view names remain valid.
SPEC.md:841:- [PY-BYTES-1] `b"..."` and `b'...'` produce managed immutable `bytes` with the `[BYTE-LITERAL-1]` escapes and decoded-size limit; a literal keeps the fixed `[N]u8` array reading only where the context demands one, including readonly `&[]u8` slice parameters. `len(b)` is signed, `b[i]` yields an `int` in 0..255 with negative indices from the end and an `R_LIST_BOUNDS` trap outside them, `b[i:j]` copies a clamped byte range, `a + b` concatenates, `==`/`!=` compare content, `in`/`not in` search a byte scalar or a contiguous subsequence, and `for x in b` yields `int` under the list iteration-bound rules. Byte strings are immutable: no operation mutates a receiver, and in-place appends belong to the separate mutable `byte_buffer` of [PY-BYTES-6]. Byte strings are traced and rooted like `str`, and construction, concatenation, slicing and conversion contribute `managed_allocation`.
SPEC.md:845:- [PY-BYTES-3] `b.split(sep)` returns `list[bytes]` and rejects an empty separator, `sep.join(parts)` rebuilds a byte string, and `b.startswith(x)`, `b.endswith(x)` and `b.find(x)` (returning -1 when absent) mirror the text operations. `b.find_any(values, start)` reads `values` as a set of octets and answers the first index at or after `start` holding any octet of that set, or -1 when none does: because `values` is a set, an empty one finds nothing and a repeated octet changes nothing; because this is a search and not an indexed read, a negative `start` clamps to 0 and a `start` at or past `len(b)` answers -1 rather than trapping. `bytes` is a prelude type usable in `list[bytes]`, `dict[str, bytes]`, record fields and `Option`/`Result` payloads; dictionary keys remain `int` or `str`. Like `str`, `bytes` is accepted in exported module signatures and rejected at JSON, host-function and native-embedding boundaries with those boundaries' existing diagnostics.
SPEC.md:847:- [PY-BYTES-6] `byte_buffer` is the mutable octet-buffer type beside the immutable `bytes`: the same one-octet elements, length, indexing, iteration, content search and content equality, but its own nominal type and a growable backing whose capacity exceeds its length. `byte_buffer(values)` builds one from a `list[int]` with the same `R_BYTE_RANGE` trap as `bytes(values)`, and only it declares the six in-place methods: `append_byte(value)` refuses a value outside 0..255, `append_range(data, start, end)` appends `data[start:end]` and refuses a negative `start`, an `end` below `start` or an `end` past `len(data)`, `append_utf8(text)` appends the UTF-8 encoding of a `str`, `append_int(value)` appends any `int`'s decimal spelling including the minimum signed value, `append_float(value)` appends the binary64's canonical spelling — byte for byte what `str(value)` prints, including the `.0` of an integral value and the `nan`/`inf`/`-inf` spellings — and `reserve(extra)` grows capacity for `extra` more octets while leaving `len` and every octet unchanged. Each answers a `bool` that is true exactly when it accepted the request, so a no-op success — `reserve(0)` or an empty range — answers true and writes nothing, while a refusal leaves the receiver byte-for-byte unchanged; each contributes `managed_allocation` and mutates only its receiver. An append writes the fragment's octets straight into spare capacity, growth doubles the backing with `O(log n)` allocations, and no append allocates a value for the fragment: `append_utf8` and `append_int` build neither a temporary `bytes` nor a temporary `str`, and both `append_int` and `append_float` write their spelling with no intermediate managed value at all — the float formatter builds the text on its own stack and appends it through the runtime's raw octet entry point. Length, indexing, iteration, comparison, search and every conversion read the buffer's current content, and identity is stable across growth, so aliases observe the same appends. Because the append methods belong only to `byte_buffer`, an immutable `bytes` literal, slice or view can never be written through, and a `byte_buffer` receiver is rejected with `E_COLLECTION_METHOD` for every one of them. `bytes(buffer)` publishes the buffer's current octets as an immutable snapshot ([PY-BYTES-SNAPSHOT-1]), so a later append cannot change an earlier snapshot; a comparison between the two byte kinds compares their octets as content.
SPEC.md:848:- [PY-BYTES-7] `b.view(start, end)` answers a `bytes` that reads the range `b[start:end]` without copying it: the endpoints clamp exactly like the slice that would have copied them, so a negative start counts from the end, an end past the length clamps, and a reversed or empty interval is the empty byte string. The view retains the source's backing storage, so its octets stay readable after the byte string that first owned them is unreachable, and a view of a view reads the same block one range further in. Every byte-string read — length, indexing, iteration, comparison, membership, search, slicing, `decode`, `scalar_count`, numeric parsing — applies to a view unchanged, and slicing one copies the range it names. `b.copy()` materializes an owned copy that shares no storage with the receiver. Views are immutable: the in-place methods belong only to `byte_buffer`, so `view` and `copy` are rejected on a mutable buffer receiver with `E_COLLECTION_METHOD` and no operation can write through a view. Constructing a view allocates its own small header and contributes `managed_allocation`; comparing, searching, counting, parsing and re-emitting a view allocate nothing.
SPEC.md:854:- [UTF8-DECODE-1] The native bytes decoder validates UTF-8 in the same pass that counts and fills scalars instead of validating first and rebuilding second. ASCII input is one high-bit scan plus one widening pass; every other input runs the validating decoder once to count and once to fill, with no separate prior validation pass. It still rejects exactly the byte sequences the former validate-then-build pair rejected — overlong encodings, surrogate code points U+D800..U+DFFF, values above U+10FFFF, truncation and stray continuation bytes — publishes the same scalar sequence for accepted input, and still separates malformed input (`MYL_BYTES_INVALID`, reported as the `Result` error text) from a refused allocation (`MYL_BYTES_MEMORY`).
SPEC.md:859:- [PYTHON-CALL-4] Keyword/default binding, source-order lowering, execution, and native emission release partial allocations on allocator failure.
SPEC.md:888:- [REFERENCE-12] An allocation failure while rendering either the text or the JSON answer reaches the caller as an out-of-memory error rather than being swallowed, rendered as a partial answer, or reported as a normal empty result.
SPEC.md:889:- [SPLIT-WHITESPACE-1] `str.split()` with no argument follows Python: it breaks the receiver on maximal runs of the same six ASCII whitespace scalars `str.isspace()` classifies with — space, tab, line feed, carriage return, vertical tab and form feed — keeps only the nonempty pieces, and answers `list[str]`, so leading, trailing and doubled whitespace produce no empty string and `"".split()` is empty. The result allocates and carries `managed_allocation`.
SPEC.md:921:- [TYPED-HOLES-5] A typed hole is reported even when its snapshot cannot be retained: a failed snapshot allocation still appends the `E_TYPED_HOLE` diagnostic carrying no obligation, and only a failure to append the diagnostic itself is `OutOfMemory`.
SPEC.md:933:- [PARSER-HINT-6] A Python statement shape whose terminator is missing names the accepted spelling at that point instead of only `unexpected token`: a chained assignment (`x = y = 21`) reports at the second `=`; `del xs[0]` reports at the first token after `del` with the alternative `remove with _ = xs.pop(index) for a list or _ = d.pop(key) for a dictionary`; a `try:` that opens a suite reports at its colon with the `Result` alternative; and an unknown string prefix (`r"a\nb"`) reports at the adjacent literal with the ordinary-string alternative. Each shape already failed to parse, so the hint chooses the message for an existing rejection and never admits a program the parser rejected before. The conditional-expression and comma-list rows are gone: `y = 42 if x > 0 else 0`, `a, b = b, a` and `return a // b, a % b` are spellings the parser now accepts.
SPEC.md:934:- [PARSER-HINT-8] The collection forms the parser once named alternatives for — a set literal, a dict comprehension, a second `for` inside a list comprehension and a generator argument — are accepted spellings now ([SET-1], [PY-GATHER-1], [PY-GENERATOR-1]), so they parse into the program they mean and the declaration table no longer lists them.
SPEC.md:937:- [DIAG-ALTERNATIVE-1] A `dict.get` call that omits the fallback reports `E_ARITY` at the call with the receiver's rendered signature and an `alternative` note that names the accepted call, because the fallback is a required operand of the one spelling `get` has: `a dictionary get always takes its fallback: d.get(key, default)`. Every other rejected arity of every other collection operation keeps its former message and its former note, so this adds a spelling where one is known and stays silent where none is.
SPEC.md:947:- [PRELUDE-SORT-KEY-2] The key slot of a lambda key in `sorted(xs, key=...)` or `values.sort(key=...)` has exactly the callback type `def(T) -> K effects(managed_allocation)` spells (LAMBDA-KEY-INFER-1), return-origin bound included, so it is one type with every other callback value of that signature in the program. A key that returns its own argument (a named function or `lambda v: v`), a key held in a local, one key used by several calls, and a callback of the same type stored, invoked, declared as a parameter or passed to `sort.sort_by_key` elsewhere in the same program all check, build and run.
SPEC.md:952:- [TEXT-VIEW-2] A view is allocated with an element descriptor whose one traced field is its owner, so the collector and the arena scan keep the owner alive through the view with no change of their own, and the budget charges a view as the 48-byte object it is. A view's hash equals an owned text's hash of the same octets and is recomputed rather than cached, because its hash word holds the offset, so a view is a dictionary key interchangeable with any text of the same octets; a dictionary stores a view key as an owned copy of its octets in its own region, so every stored key reads its octets from its own block and a key never keeps a larger text alive. A piece made inside `with arena()` belongs to the innermost arena exactly as a copy did — the receiver is reused as a whole piece only when it already lives there — so the store barrier traps `R_ARENA_ESCAPE` exactly where it did, a view never lives in a shallower arena than its owner, and `escape` copies a view into an owned text of its own octets.
SPEC.md:957:- [TEXT-INDEX-3] Every constructor that knows the scalar count of the text it builds — decoding a byte string, concatenation, `join`, `replace`, case mapping, `zfill`, a slice, an escape copy, a stepped slice, a dictionary's copy of a key piece — allocates the index with the text and publishes an exact ASCII flag (a text whose scalar count equals its octet count is ASCII whatever produced it, so `replace("é", "e")` answers text that indexes by octet), and decoding a mostly ASCII byte string validates whole words of ASCII at a time. Results, the `R_TEXT_BOUNDS` trap and its detail, negative indices, slice clamping, immutability, equality, ordering and hashing are those of a text with no index.
SPEC.md:958:- [LIST-SORT-RUNS-1] `values.sort()` — and `sorted`, `sort.sort_ints`, `sort.sort_floats` and `sort.sort_strs`, which sort a copy with it — is an adaptive stable merge in the native runtime: an ascending run already in the list is kept, a strictly descending run is reversed, a run shorter than the minimum run length, between 16 and 32 elements, is extended by insertion, and runs merge in powersort order through one scratch buffer of at most half the list, so an ordered, reversed or run-structured list sorts in `O(n)` and every list in `O(n log n)`. Each element type has its own comparison and moves — signed and unsigned 64-bit integers, binary64 values and `str` — and the narrower integer widths share one generic path. The order is exactly PY-COLLECTIONS-15's: integers by value, every NaN after every ordered float, equal values and signed zeros in input order, `str` by Unicode scalar value. The list keeps its identity, length, capacity and generation, every alias sees the sorted elements, and a sort allocates nothing managed, so no collection runs while it holds elements in its scratch buffer.
SPEC.md:959:- [PY-DICT-2] A managed dictionary keeps an open-addressing hash index beside its insertion-ordered entry list, so reading, writing, `in`, `setdefault` and `update` are expected constant time in the entry count instead of a scan. The index is an untraced managed block owned by the dictionary's own region, reached through a traced field, so collection keeps it alive with the dictionary, an arena releases the two together and its bytes are charged to the memory budget. Hashing reads exactly the units equality compares — a `str` key's UTF-8 octets, any other key's bytes ([PY-DICT-3]) — with fixed constants and no seed, so a dictionary behaves identically on every run. Every observable answer is unchanged: iteration, `keys`/`values`/`items`, snapshots and printing stay in insertion order, structural equality still ignores insertion order, and `pop` removes its key without moving any other entry ([DICT-REMOVE-1]). An arena escape copies the entry list first and hashes the clone's keys only once every cloned key holds its final contents; a refused index allocation leaves the dictionary answering by scanning rather than failing the write.
SPEC.md:960:- [PY-DICT-3] A dictionary key is hashed a word at a time. A scalar key's bytes, zero-extended to one 64-bit word, are hashed by one full 64×64-bit multiply whose high and low halves are xored together and folded to 32 bits. A `str` key's UTF-8 payload is read eight octets at a time — a payload of at most eight octets as two overlapping halves folded once, a longer one word by word into a state seeded by its length, ending on the word that finishes at its last octet, so no read passes the payload and every octet reaches the answer — and the result is cached in the text's header. Both use fixed constants and no seed. Equal keys have equal bytes and therefore equal hashes, and no observable answer depends on the hash: insertion order, iteration, `keys`/`values`/`items`, snapshots, printing, equality and every lookup answer exactly as they did under the octet-at-a-time FNV-1a hash. A `float` key is refused ([HASH-KEYS-1]), so there is no `0.0`/`-0.0` or NaN key case, and every other key type hashes structurally ([HASH-KEYS-2]).
SPEC.md:961:- [PY-DICT-4] Every get-then-set spelling probes the table once for every key type. `counts[k] = counts.get(k, 0) + 1`, `counts[k] += 1` and `if k in counts: counts[k] = …` lower to separate runtime calls on the same key, and the dictionary remembers what its last probe learned: a found entry for any key — a `str` key's object pointer, an integer key's bytes — accepted only while the live entry at the recorded position still holds the queried key, and an integer key's absence together with the empty slot a write of it would fill, so a write after a missed read inserts without walking the table again. Every insertion, table growth or refusal, `pop`, `clear` and arena reindex drops a recorded absence first. A `str` key object's absence is never recorded, because a collected text's address can be reused by an equal text that is present (a described slice's absence is, dated by the heap's collection count — [DICT-RANGE-KEY-2]); the write after a missed `str` read walks the table again, comparing only stored hashes. `setdefault` probes once for either outcome. Lookups, insertion order, defaults, traps and arena ownership are unchanged. A successful ordinary probe publishes only its key, entry and found state; fields used only by absent or described-span states retain their previous bytes. Every consumer checks its exact state, and every publisher initializes the fields required by that state. Found-entry revalidation, scalar absent slot/hash, span offset/length and GC epoch, invalidation, ownership, allocation and refusal behavior remain unchanged. Reads and `get` of dictionaries whose key and value are each `i64` or `u64` may select a scalar-word probe. It retains the same key and output ABI, validates the ordinary found nomination against live stored bits, and falls back to the complete read for unsupported runtime shapes or scan-only storage. Missing output bytes, defaults, failure provenance, ownership and accounting are unchanged.
SPEC.md:962:- [PY-DICT-5] Each index slot packs its key's 32-bit hash above one past the entry index it names, still eight bytes per slot, so a probe loads an entry only when the stored hash equals the queried key's and a table grows by re-placing its slots from their stored hashes without reading or rehashing a key. `pop` empties its key's slot by shifting the rest of the collision run back, so every remaining key stays reachable from its home slot, and leaves the entry for `settle` ([DICT-REMOVE-1]): it allocates nothing and hashes at most the popped key. A slot names at most 2³²−2 entries; a longer entry list drops the index and scans, exactly as a refused index allocation does.
SPEC.md:964:- [HASH-KEYS-1] A dictionary key or set element type must be hashable: every integer kind, `bool`, `unit`, `str`, `bytes`, and a tuple, record, union, enum, domain, `Option`, `Result` or fixed array whose parts are all hashable. `float` (NaN is not equal to itself), a `list`, `dict`, `set` or `byte_buffer` (a shared reference whose contents can change after it is stored), a function value (no equality) and a capability, file or resource are refused where the dictionary or set is built — a dict literal, a set literal, `set()`, `set(iterable)` and a set comprehension — with `E_DICT_KEY` for a key and `E_SET_ELEMENT` for an element, whose message names the key type, the path to the first offending part (`.field`, `[index]` for a tuple element, `.case` for a union payload, `[]` for an array element) and the reason. In a generic template a concrete key type follows the same rule, `str` is accepted, and a type parameter must promise `integer`.
SPEC.md:965:- [HASH-KEYS-2] A key that is neither a `str` nor a plain scalar — a record, tuple, union, enum, domain, `Option`, fixed array, `bytes`, or any value holding a `str` — is hashed and compared by walking its descriptor: scalar bytes a word at a time, a `str` through its cached text hash and its octets, `bytes` through the octet hash a text of the same octets gets and its octets, each field in declaration order, a union's case tag and then only that case's payload, and each array element. Padding and an inactive case's bytes are never read, the constants are fixed and there is no seed, so equal values hash equally on every run and the comparison is exactly `==`. A stored key is a copy, so a later field assignment to the binding it came from cannot change the table, and dictionary equality looks each key up in the other table instead of scanning it.
SPEC.md:966:- [DICT-REMOVE-1] Removing a key from a dictionary or set (`dict.pop`, `set.remove`, `set.discard`, `set.pop`) takes its slot out of the index and moves no other entry: an entry in the middle of the entry list is only counted dead, its bytes kept and traced until `settle` squeezes every dead entry out in insertion order, renumbering the slots of the survivors in place without allocating; the last entry is cut off together with every dead entry before it, so the last entry is always live. Every positional reader — iteration, `key_at`/`value_at`, key and value snapshots, printing, equality, the source of `update` and `copy`, and the arena escape copier — settles first; an insertion settles once dead entries are at least half the list, and a table about to grow or be dropped settles before it does. `len` counts live entries. A dictionary whose index was refused still shifts later entries down.
SPEC.md:967:- [SET-1] `set[T]` is a dictionary-kind collection from its hashable elements to unit, kept in first-insertion order and independent on assignment. `{a, b}` builds one in source order (a first element followed by `,`, `}` or `for` starts a set literal or a set comprehension, and `{}` stays the empty dict); `set()` builds an empty set of the type the context expects and is `E_SET_INFERENCE` without one; `set(iterable)` copies a list, `str`, `bytes`, fixed array, dictionary's keys, dictionary view, `range(...)` or set; `{value for name in source if condition}` is the set comprehension. The methods are `add`, `remove` (a missing element traps `R_MISSING_KEY`), `discard`, `pop` (removes and answers the most recently added live element; an empty set traps `R_MISSING_KEY`), `clear`, `copy`, `update`, `union`, `intersection`, `difference`, `symmetric_difference`, `issubset`, `issuperset` and `isdisjoint`, and `union` is accepted as a member name after a dot although it is a declaration keyword. `update`, `union`, `intersection`, `difference`, `symmetric_difference`, `issubset`, `issuperset` and `isdisjoint` take another set or any iterable `set(...)` accepts, which they collect into a set first, as Python's methods do (`s.union([4])`); the operators still take two sets. `in`, `len`, `for`, `enumerate`, `list`, `sorted`, `sum`, `min`, `max`, `any` and `all` see the elements in insertion order, and a resize during iteration traps `R_LIST_ITERATION_MUTATION`. A set prints as `{1, 2}` and as `set()` when empty, wherever it is nested. `{}` against a set type is `E_DICT_TYPE`, indexing a set is `E_SET_INDEX`, and any other method is `E_LIST_METHOD` listing the set methods. A function that changes a set parameter declares it `mut`. A program's own function named `set` keeps its name and its calls beside the builtin type; in such a module set literals still build sets but the type spelling `set[T]` is not available.
SPEC.md:971:- [LAMBDA-KEY-INFER-1] A one-parameter sort-key lambda (`sorted(values, key=lambda v: ...)`, `values.sort(key=...)`) whose body is not one of the shapes LIST-SORT-KEY-2 reads takes the body's ordinary checked type as its key type: the body is checked once with its parameter bound to the list's element type and no expected result, and every trace of that trial (typed nodes, statements, locals, resolved entries, the parameter binding, option facts, ownership state and diagnostics) is undone before the lambda is checked for real against the slot `def(T) -> K effects(managed_allocation)` it fixes. The slot admits allocation because its one call site is the keyed sort, which the enclosing function is already charged `managed_allocation` for, so a key that builds a `str` (`p.name.lower()`, a slice, a helper that concatenates) is exact rather than exempt; a declared key function with a narrower ceiling still fills it. So a method call, a call to a declared function, a conversion, a slice or a conditional expression gives a `str`, `float` or integer key as written, where it used to be typed `int` and fail with `expected int, found str`. A body that does not type on its own is one `E_SORT_KEY` at the lambda that quotes the underlying diagnostic and says to write a top-level key function with a declared result; a key type with no order is still LIST-SORT-KEY-2's `E_SORT_KEY`.
SPEC.md:972:- [LIST-SORT-KEY-3] A keyed sort lowers to one key list sized to the list, filled by one pass that calls the key once per element, and one native `myl_native_list_sort_keyed` call. A numeric key becomes an order-preserving 64-bit word — the sign bit flipped, binary64 bits reflected with signed zeros merged and every NaN the largest word, all complemented for `reverse` — and when the words span fewer distinct values than there are elements, a stable counting sort gives each element, in input order, the next slot of its key; otherwise, when every word minus the smallest fits above the position bits, `(word - smallest) << bits | position` words are sorted by the integer engine in the key list's own storage; keys spread wider take two such sorts of 32 bits each. Text and tuple keys sort (key, position) entries with a stable merge. For `values.sort(...)` the elements then move once along the permutation's cycles through element-sized temporaries, prefetching a few steps ahead on each cycle, so the extra memory is the key list plus the sort's scratch and no second list of elements exists; for `sorted(values, ...)` they are gathered in order from the original list into a new list allocated at the list's length, so the copy is never built only to be permuted. The native sort calls no guest code and allocates nothing managed; scratch memory the host refuses is the allocation-failure trap, raised before any element moves.
SPEC.md:992:- [MUT-EXCLUSIVE-1] At one call the places named by any two `mut` arguments must be disjoint, or the call is `E_MUTATION_OVERLAP`. A `mut` argument names a place: a root variable plus a path of steps, each a field name or a list, fixed-array or dictionary index. Two places overlap when they have the same root and every pair of steps compared in order may be equal until one path ends, so a variable overlaps each of its parts and a place overlaps itself. Two field steps are equal exactly when they name the same field. Two index or key steps are provably distinct only when both are compile-time constants (an integer or bool literal or a scalar `const`, a negated integer literal, a string or bytes literal, a payload-free enum case; arithmetic such as `1 + 1` is not folded) that differ; a negative list or array index counts back from the end, so `xs[-1]` and `xs[2]` may name one element while two different negative constants, or two different non-negative ones, are distinct, and two different constant dictionary keys are distinct whatever their signs. Anything else may be equal. So `f(mut b, mut b)`, `f(mut a, mut a[0])`, `f(mut r, mut r.rows)`, `f(mut xs[i], mut xs[j])`, `f(mut xs[0], mut xs[0])` and `f(mut xs[-1], mut xs[2])` are rejected, while `f(mut s.a, mut s.b)`, `f(mut xs[0], mut xs[2])`, `f(mut rows[i].a, mut rows[j].b)`, `f(mut d["a"], mut d["b"])` and `f(mut rt, mut o)` stay legal. The rule makes two `mut` parameters of one call disjoint, which the copy analysis and the RC optimizer assume.
SPEC.md:1006:- [TUPLE-2] `(a, b)` and the bare forms after `return` build a tuple value from elements checked in source order, with the expected-type rule for literals: when the destination already demands `tuple[T1, T2]` each element is checked against its positional type, and otherwise the element types are inferred and interned. A tuple may be stored in a list, as a dictionary value, in a record field, in an `Option` or `Result` payload, and nested in another tuple; it is managed-bearing exactly when one of its elements is, and a value whose arity differs from the demanded tuple type is `E_TUPLE_ARITY` naming the count it has and the count it needs.
SPEC.md:1008:- [PY-DESTRUCTURE-PLACE-1] A destructuring target list may name elements and fields as well as names: `a[i], a[j] = a[j], a[i]` swaps two elements of a fixed array or list, and `d[k], r.f, xs[-1].g, name = value` writes a dictionary entry, a record field, a field of an element and a binding. The value is evaluated once, before any target, into one temporary whose arity is checked as `TUPLE-3` says; then each target in order evaluates its own receiver and indices and takes its element exactly as `target = element` would, with the same type, mutability, `mut`-parameter, arena and negative-index rules. A target that is not a name, element or field is `E_ASSIGNMENT_TARGET`.
SPEC.md:1010:- [TUPLE-5] A tuple has no elements to index, no fields, no methods and no `len`: `pair[0]` is `E_TUPLE_INDEX` naming the destructuring alternative, `pair.head` and `pair.count()` are `E_TYPE` naming the same alternative, and `len(pair)` is `E_TYPE` naming it too. A tuple type compared against a different tuple type is `E_TYPE` through the ordinary expected/found message, dictionary keys stay `int` and `str` so a tuple is never a key, `DictItem`, `zip` and `enumerate` are unchanged, `match` patterns over tuples are not accepted, and the `extern def` and JSON boundaries reject a tuple graph like any other non-data graph.
SPEC.md:1019:- [DERIVED-EFFECTS-1] A function's effect ceiling is derived from its parameter list: a `Stream` parameter permits `output`, `Filesystem` `filesystem`, `Network` `network`, `Clock` `clock`, `Entropy` `random`, `Environment` `environment`, `Host` `host`, a `mut` parameter `mutation`, a callback parameter the bounded ceiling its type declares, and `managed_allocation` is always permitted; the ownership a parameter or the result type carries contributes the effect of the resource family it holds, because a value the body may drop is authority the signature already grants. A body that exceeds that ceiling is rejected in that body with `E_EFFECT_BOUND` at the operation or call that reaches the effect, and the message names the parameter to add (`add `out: Stream` to the parameters`). A `host def` or `extern def` has no body to derive from, so its clause stays its ceiling as written, and a callback type whose ceiling is omitted is unbounded rather than a promise: it contributes nothing to the derived set and an invoke through such a value contributes nothing either, because the value's own signature is where that authority is declared.
SPEC.md:1020:- [DERIVED-EFFECTS-2] `noalloc`, `noio` and `effects(...)` survive only as narrowings of the derived ceiling. A clause that permits exactly what the parameters imply — `effects(host, managed_allocation)` on a `Host` parameter, or `noio` on a body with no io-capable parameter — is `W_DERIVED_EFFECTS` at the clause, whose message quotes the clause and says to remove it; `noalloc` never restates, because allocation is always permitted. The warning blocks under `lint --profile=strict`, where it is the `derived-effects` rule.
SPEC.md:1022:- [DERIVED-EFFECTS-4] The published `ceiling` is the derived set narrowed by an explicit clause, for a declaration whose body was never checked as much as for a checked one: `list-interfaces` prints `ceiling(host,managed_allocation)` from the parameter list alone beside `analysis(declaration-only)`, the contract manifest's `permissions` and the public schema publish it, and `interface_sha256` carries it in place of the inferred mask. The published `effects` fact stays the checked body fact, so a body-only edit that adds an allocation inside the derived ceiling changes `body_sha256` and leaves every declaration interface and caller type check untouched.
SPEC.md:1048:- [VALUE-SEMANTICS-2] Under default value semantics a `list`, `dict`, `set` or `byte_buffer`, and every record, tuple, union payload, `Option`/`Result` or fixed array holding one, is an independent value. Assignment to a named local (a `for` variable included), a store into a record field, tuple, payload, fixed-array element, list element (`append`, `insert`, `xs[i] = v`), dictionary value (`d[k] = v`, `setdefault`), the elements an `extend`, `update` or keyed `sorted` takes from another collection, a named `match` payload binding of a value another name holds, the default a `get` or `pop` may hand back, and a `return` each give the destination its own value: lowering places one `value_copy` after every such transfer that is not fresh (a literal, constructor, call result, `pop`, `escape`), and the ownership analysis (`OWNERSHIP-ANALYSIS-1`) drops it where the transfer is a move (the source is never read again) or a borrow (neither side is written while both are read). A local that may hold a shared value — a parameter, a compiler temporary filled from a place, a local some store filled with a borrow — never hands its value over. A shallow copy, slice or concatenation of a collection whose elements hold collections copies those elements too. A read-only argument or other operand is lent, not copied: the callee cannot write it, and where a later operand of the same expression may write it, it is copied when it is evaluated instead (`VALUE-SEMANTICS-14`). Every real copy shallow-copies its outer value into private scratch and marks each directly reachable mutable collection payload shared: fixed flat values mark up to eight leaves inline, while dynamic and recursive shapes use rooted `myl_qbe_cow_transfer`. A later write clones shared storage in its payload's owning region; a pinned payload is cloned at transfer. When an inner arena is open around a write to an outer value, using the owner's region prevents the replacement from dangling at arena exit. Immutable `str` and `bytes` stay shared; `class` objects and Cells exist only under `--legacy-sharing`. A copy is not a `managed_allocation` effect in this stage, and `.api` interface files do not change.
SPEC.md:1056:- [VALUE-SEMANTICS-7] Under default value semantics `old(expr)` of a value whose writable storage is all collections (a `list`, `dict`, `set`, `byte_buffer` or a record of them) is accepted: the capture is a value of its own.
SPEC.md:1057:- [VALUE-SEMANTICS-8] Documentation examples use the default value model. `# doc: value-semantics` explicitly selects that model and `# doc: legacy-sharing` selects the migration release's legacy model, for both checking and execution, including grouped projects. The doctest runner passes the corresponding CLI mode flag to child commands; contradictory directives fail through the CLI's multiple-mode rejection.
SPEC.md:1073:- [D1-LIVE-PLACE] Iteration over a place rooted at a local or parameter sees element writes and mut-callee writes through that place, including record-field write-back, fixed-array places, comprehensions and generators. Produced expressions, filters and nested clauses retain the same live-source rule. Indices are evaluated once at entry; every indexed managed ancestor's length is checked exactly each step, including ancestors of immutable str/bytes. A live list or byte buffer sees growth and traps a decrease from its last observed length, including after earlier growth; dictionaries, sets and immutable str/bytes terminals trap any observed length change. Same-length str/bytes field replacement is visible; text length and indexing count Unicode scalars. Fixed-array terminal lengths are static. Direct rebinding of the root in the body selects an independent entry snapshot; non-place sources keep their entry behavior. Guards run before each element and after each continued or final iteration, while break exits without another guard.
SPEC.md:1082:- [MUT-REBIND-CALLER-1] A whole-variable rebinding of a `mut` list, dictionary or set parameter reaches the caller, including a rebinding in a loop, on some paths only, or through recursive forwarding. Writes before and after the rebinding reach that variable; read-only sources and named snapshots remain independent. Fixed-array rebinding remains E_ASSIGNMENT_TARGET (PY-ARRAY-MUT-1).
SPEC.md:1096:- [MUT-EXPRESSION-STACK-1] A transparent `mut` argument marker consumes exactly one checked target expression, regardless of whether that target lowers to an indexed read, a field or a synthesized block. Nested dictionary mutation arguments compose with later call arguments without checker stack underflow, while missing-marker and nonassignable-target diagnostics remain unchanged.
SPEC.md:1142:- [EMBED-1] `embed_bytes("path")` answers `bytes` and `embed_str("path")` answers `str`, both read from a project file while the program loads, so the data is immutable static content of the binary. They are prelude builtins that a binding, declaration or module name may shadow. Each takes exactly one plain string literal (a variable, `b"..."`, a second argument or a computed path is `E_EMBED_ARGUMENT`), and `embed_str` requires valid UTF-8 (`E_EMBED_UTF8` names the offending byte offset). A call needs no capability parameter and contributes no effect, not even `managed_allocation`, so it is legal under `noio`, `noalloc` and `effects()`, in generic bodies, and as the whole initializer of a module `const` whose type is `bytes` for `embed_bytes` and `str` for `embed_str`; reading that constant, locally or through an import, loads static data and allocates nothing. Every byte is kept, including NUL, and an empty file is an empty value. Native embedded objects are ordered by semantic symbol in both whole-program emission and module linking. Behavior is identical under reference counting, `--gc-runtime` and `--release`.
SPEC.md:1144:- [EMBED-3] The loader finds every `embed_bytes("...")` and `embed_str("...")` call while it reads a module's header, reads each file once with the project provider, and records its outcome and BLAKE3 content digest on the module's source, so the checker only looks results up and a failed read is an ordinary diagnostic. That digest enters every key that covers the module: the configured identity the frontend verdict, in-memory verdict, run-manifest and emit-replay caches key on; the dependency context of each declaration whose region holds an embed, which keys checked bodies, check summaries, stand-in units and stored fragments; the implementation hash of a native test; and the test snapshot's resolved identity and materialized copy, where captured files sit at the paths their relative references need. Editing only an embedded file therefore rechecks and re-emits exactly the declarations that embed it, an unchanged file reuses everything, and deleting or replacing a file with a directory changes the keys as well.
SPEC.md:1145:- [EMBED-5] A `project-dependencies-v1` receipt lists every embedded file as `{module, path, status, blake3, bytes}` beside the module inputs and `--explain-invalidation` reports an edit to one at the `load_source` stage. `mylang fmt` leaves an embed call as written, and `mylang check` and `check-session` report embed diagnostics and never replay a verdict across an edit, deletion or replacement of an embedded file. An interface file (`mylang api`) prints an embedded constant as its initializer text, `embed_bytes("path")`, never the file's bytes, so editing the file leaves the interface unchanged.
SPEC.md:1149:- [DISCARD-NAME-1] `_` is the discard name and never shadows anything. A `for` target (`for _ in ...`, `for _, x in ...`, `for _, _ in ...`, the second name of `enumerate`, `zip` and dictionary items included), a comprehension element name and a `with ... as _` scope may bind `_` again inside another scope that binds it, so `for _ in range(2):` around `for _ in range(3):` is legal, and a loop that discards both of its names binds nothing. The inner `_` hides the outer one for its own scope and the outer one is visible again after it. `_` does not become an error to read where it is bound today, and a `match` payload of `_` still binds nothing. Every other name keeps the rule: a `for` variable, comprehension element or payload binding may not reuse an active local or top-level name (`E_SHADOWING`), because loop variables are block-scoped.
SPEC.md:1151:- [CONST-METHOD-1] `NAME.method(args)` on a module constant declared `str` or `bytes` is checked as the same method on a local bound to the constant: the constant is read once through its getter and the method applies to that value, in ordinary and generic function bodies, for an `embed_bytes`/`embed_str` constant, for a private constant of the same module and for a public one reached through an import (`alias.NAME.method(args)`). The method vocabulary, arities, diagnostics and effects are the local receiver's: a literal constant contributes `managed_allocation`, an embedded one none. A constant of another type has no methods.
SPEC.md:1162:- [PRELUDE-REPR-1] The shadowable one-argument `repr` builtin answers exact CPython scalar spelling for str, bytes, every integer width, float and bool, with Unicode16.0.0 quote/printability/escape rules and shortest binary64 exponent layout, including -0.0 and unsigned64 maximum. The operand evaluates once and unsupported collections/records are E_REPR_TYPE. Ordinary print/str formatting stays separate; a large non-ASCII repr result carries the TEXT-INDEX position index and inputs/views are rooted across its single result allocation.
SPEC.md:1166:- [CLI-CACHE-ISOLATION-1] Native artifact location is independent of frontend reuse: `build --no-cache --cache-dir DIR` uses DIR, `test --no-cache` stages native objects inside its owned scratch snapshot, and doctest keeps all scratch and child caches under TMPDIR (or the host temporary directory), without writing the documentation root or caller's default cache.
SPEC.md:1171:- [EMBED-PROJECT-BUDGET-1] Project max_file_bytes and max_total_bytes bound embedded inputs as well as source inputs. The loader passes each remaining read allowance to the provider before allocation, charges each successful module/path embedded identity once against the total, and reports E_PROJECT_BYTES at the embed literal when a project allowance is exceeded. The independent 16 MiB embed cap retains E_EMBED_TOO_LARGE when it is the limiting bound.
SPEC.md:1224:- [NEW-CORE-LLVM-2] A release build emits a proven `+ - *` whose operands and result the facts prove non-negative as `nuw nsw`, a comparison of two proven non-negative values with unsigned predicates, and proven floor division as `udiv`; verify builds do none of this. Release code generation aligns innermost loops to 64 bytes (the target description's flags), so a small hot loop sits in one fetch and prediction window.
SPEC.md:1228:- [NEW-CORE-KERNELS-1] The S0 compute kernels as single-kernel programs under `test/core` (`float_math`, `call_direct`, `branch_predictable`, `branch_mixed`, `binary_search`, kinds 1, 6, 2, 3 and 10 of `benchmarks/runtime/expanded/compute/main.my`) build at debug and release and print the old core's checksums at 4096 and 64 elements; floats, list[bool] elements, helper calls and a data-dependent while loop over a list parameter are covered.
SPEC.md:1240:- [NEW-CORE-RC-8] A list released at its death whose origin is fresh (an allocation, a copy, an update, or phis of those) and that no call received and no retained use shared is freed without its count test (a `release` with `data = 1`); verify builds assert `count == 1` there and trap `R_CORE_FACT` (`count == 1 before freeing a unique block`). A list a call received keeps the test, since the callee may have kept it.
SPEC.md:1254:- [NEW-CORE-RECORD-2] The Core IR builder lowers a checked `record` to `record_make` (fields evaluated in the order written, placed in declaration order), a field read to `record_get` and a field write (`update`) to `record_set`; a nested record is a field whose type is a record, so `a.b.c = v` reads `a.b`, replaces `c` and replaces `b` in `a`. A `class` or a record holding a fixed array, dictionary, bytes or function value is refused with that reason, and a type that failed to map is refused again at its next use.
SPEC.md:1267:- [NEW-CORE-SORT-1] The new core lowers `xs.sort()` and `sorted(xs)` for integers, floats, strings and bytes to consuming `list_sort` with one-count copy-on-write, then the independent runtime's adaptive stable powersort (`myl_core_s2_sort_in_place`): natural runs, stable insertion and shorter-run scratch. Aliases retain their original order; integers order by value, floats put NaNs last and tie signed zeros, text and bytes compare their octets. Keyed and reverse sorting compute each key once in source order, then `myl_core_s2_sort_keyed` uses counting or packed-word ordering for scalar numeric keys and a stable merge for text, bytes and flat tuple keys. It receives the exact source/key strides, preserves equal-key input order in both directions and returns an owning sorted copy. Unsupported element or key types receive E_CORE_UNSUPPORTED.
SPEC.md:1282:- [NEW-CORE-TEST-2] `mylang-core test` builds one executable per file, at debug or with `--release`, whose synthesized entry parses its first argument as the index of a test in the runner's table and calls it with a stand-in for each `Stream` parameter; the runner runs every test as its own process, so a trap, a crash or a timeout (30 s) ends only that test, and reads the verdict from the exit status (0 passed, 2 returned false) and the trap report on stderr.
SPEC.md:1299:- [NEW-CORE-S2-IR-1] S2 Core IR provides layout-independent dictionary operations, bytes, inline fixed arrays, top-level function values and SSA-only string spans; every new operation verifies its operand and result types. Dictionary insert probes originate from a lookup of the same dictionary and key. String spans cannot be stored, merged or returned and are admitted only by designated borrowing operations or explicit extern-C span parameters. Consuming updates use copy-on-write under the existing one-count ownership rules. Until source lowering is implemented the builder reports E_CORE_UNSUPPORTED; unfinished hand-built IR operations trap E_CORE_UNSUPPORTED before any result is observable.
SPEC.md:1303:- [NEW-CORE-TEXT-BORROW-1] Text encode changes the pointer type to bytes without allocation or count traffic when borrowed. Byte uses keep their source alive; escaping byte values retain the source, so early source release and COW writes preserve exact values.
SPEC.md:1308:- [NEW-CORE-DICT-1] The new core lowers insertion-ordered dictionaries and unit-valued sets through pure SSA probes, consuming COW updates, borrowed entry reads, deletion tombstones and order-independent equality. Hybrid 16-slot control probes and cached hashes preserve key equality; missing required keys report R_MISSING_KEY. Immutable dictionary lengths and scalar key/value hulls are asserted by fact-verification builds. Unknown call and parameter inputs settle to unknown facts through copies and updates, weakening loop joins. Direct views retain precise mapping identity during their immediate consumer; D2-VIEW-STORAGE-1 rejects source storage before new-core lowering. Internal view origins remain conservatively guarded.
SPEC.md:1309:- [NEW-CORE-DICT-SMALL-1] A dictionary or set probe on a table of at most eight entries (which has no index) scans the cached entry hashes inline and compares a key only on a hash match, with no runtime call; a table that ever lost an entry, and so carries a deletion bitmap, asks `myl_core_dict_find` instead. A miss answers -1 either way.
SPEC.md:1326:- [NEW-CORE-S3-VALUES-1] Recursive equality and dictionary-key hashing use explicit worklists and shared-node memo tables; padding and inactive union payloads do not participate, and identical pointers do not bypass NaN inequality. Aggregate text preserves nominal record and variant names, tuple positions, insertion order and the existing depth-24 ellipsis rule. List repetition creates independent values under one-count ownership.
SPEC.md:1343:- [NEW-CORE-S4-UNITS-1] Independent source-module, generic, adapter and entry units have deterministic LLVM text with stable program symbols and explicit fastcc calls. Reached strings, layouts, dictionary and value/render descriptors, zeros and metadata use local first-use numbering. Unrelated edits preserve unit text; consumed inline bodies and facts change it. Native linking unions number and math dependencies over every unit.
SPEC.md:1354:- [NEW-CORE-S4-CAP-FIELD-1] A capability token (`Stream`, `Host`, a provider capability) may be a record field or payload in the new core: it is an `i64` with no count, so ownership and authority rules are unchanged; fixed arrays of them follow NEW-CORE-S5-CAP-COLLECTIONS-1, and the checker rejects lists and dictionaries of them (`E_LIST_CAPABILITY`) in both cores.
SPEC.md:1356:- [NEW-CORE-S4-MEMORY-1] `mylang-core test --max-memory SIZE` (bytes or a `K`/`M`/`G` size; else `MYLANG_MEMORY_LIMIT`, else `limits.test.max_memory`, else 512 MiB; `0` removes it) bounds each test process: the runtime counts the chunks small blocks are carved from and every live large block, and an allocation past the ceiling traps `R_MEMORY_BUDGET`; the process's `RLIMIT_AS` is lowered (never raised) to the ceiling plus 256 MiB, and an allocation the OS then refuses reports `R_MEMORY_BUDGET` too. The ceiling and the frame budget are part of the test receipt key. Programs built outside tests carry no ceiling and pay nothing on the inline allocation paths.
SPEC.md:1377:- [NEW-CORE-S5-RUNTIME-LAYOUT-1] The core runtime object compiles with `-falign-functions=64`, and its stack-floor signal stack is mapped when the program starts rather than reserved as a 256 KiB static array. Every runtime function starts on a cache line, so a change to one runtime function no longer moves the others' alignment, and no large `.bss` moves where the heap begins. Before this, unrelated runtime edits moved allocation- and text-heavy rows by 5–10% (S5-X: `packet_decode`, `csv_aggregate`, with identical instruction counts and more branch misses).
SPEC.md:1379:- [NEW-CORE-S5-VIEW-LEN-1] `len()` of a dictionary's `keys()` or `values()` view, or of any buffer operation typed `int`, is an `int` like `len(d)`: the IR's `u64` length is converted exactly, so the value can be stored, added or returned from `main`; it was an invalid-IR internal error before.
SPEC.md:1397:- [MYLANG-START-1] `mylang` starts with the allocator, I/O, arena and environment Zig's start code would give `main`, except that the Debug and ReleaseSafe allocator records no stack trace per allocation. It keeps Zig's leak, double-free and write-after-free checks. The first stack trace parsed and sorted the unwind tables of the whole executable: about 17 ms of every Debug `mylang` start, two thirds of a cached `mylang run`.
SPEC.md:1413:- [FRONTEND-COST-1] `mylang cost ENTRY.my [FUNCTION] [--json]` reports static Core costs per reachable emitted function and their totals: kept/proved checks, in-place/tested/copied updates, potential dynamic allocation sites, retains/releases, inlining and vector plans. Totals share the `build --stats` counters. Cost and `build --stats` collect LLVM optimization remarks from identical emitted units; LLVM vectorization is unknown without positive optimization remarks; `--llvm-remarks FILE.opt.yaml` reads successful LLVM loop-vectorization remarks separately from Core vector plans. Allocation classification includes runtime scratch, counts unresolved function values and foreign calls conservatively, and is shared with the build-time allocation promise. `--debug`, `--no-facts` and `--verify-facts` select the corresponding build pipeline.
